# team-networking: ring count vs max_worker_processes
#
# A/B of the eventfd ring pool against the 1:1 semaphore ring. The driver is
# bench/internals/threads/ring_scaling.py: N processes, one ring each, each
# doing 3 object-free reads (Cursor.aggregate: columnar batches reduced in C++,
# no Python object per row), 100k rows per read, batch 25k. It is bind-mounted
# so the SAME file runs on both images (the baseline image predates it).
#
# Six pairs of container runs were interleaved under /tmp/fpq-bench.lock (one
# docker run at a time), 3 reps per setting per run; the tables below are the
# medians of the six. An earlier single pair at 5 reps agrees with them.
# Host: Ryzen 7 7700, 8C/16T, 30 GiB, ~2 cores of background load (desktop);
# that is the source of the +/-5-10% cell-to-cell noise at n<=4.

### COMMAND (baseline)
flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
  -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline \
  -v $PWD/bench/internals/threads/ring_scaling.py:/tmp/ring_scaling.py:ro \
  fpq-baseline python3 /tmp/ring_scaling.py --rows 100000 --reads 3 --reps 3'

### COMMAND (change)
flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
  -e FASTPOSTGRES_TRANSPORT=eventfd -e BENCH_GIT_REV=networking \
  -v $PWD/bench/internals/threads/ring_scaling.py:/tmp/ring_scaling.py:ro \
  fpq-networking python3 /tmp/ring_scaling.py --rows 100000 --reads 3 --reps 3'

# Baseline: main@7e3d2e6, image fpq-baseline. Change: team/networking,
# image fpq-networking built from this worktree under the same lock.

### baseline (fpq-baseline, shm engine)

### ENV
cpu: AMD Ryzen 7 7700 8-Core Processor
mem: 30 GiB
ghz: 3.8
cores: 8
postgres: 18.6
python: 3.13.5
sqlalchemy: 2.0.40
pyarrow: 26.0.0
adbc: 1.13.0
shape: bench
git: baseline
shm: 3.0G
### END ENV

### RING-SCALING
rows: 100000
reads: 3 per process
reps: 3 per run, 6 runs interleaved (median reported)
batch: 25000
shape: fp_ring_scaling_t (the benchmark's six-column row)
transport: shm
rings_per_worker: <default>
shm_pool: <default>
git: baseline
### END RING-SCALING

 n served ring workers   aggregate rows/s   per reader
 1      1            1          6,539,803    6,539,803
 2      2            2         12,757,899    6,378,950
 4      4            4         23,439,044    5,859,761
 8      7            7         31,113,297    4,444,757 (1 reader had no ring)
# The n=8 row is 7 readers: with max_worker_processes=8 and one held back the
# eighth process gets no background worker. Its aggregate() raises; a DB-API
# execute() would silently read over libpq. Raw n=8 values across the six runs:
# 31.17M, 29.61M, 31.63M, 28.79M, 32.86M, 31.05M.

### change (fpq-networking, eventfd engine, default policy)

### ENV
cpu: AMD Ryzen 7 7700 8-Core Processor
mem: 30 GiB
ghz: 3.8
cores: 8
postgres: 18.6
python: 3.13.5
sqlalchemy: 2.0.40
pyarrow: 26.0.0
adbc: 1.13.0
shape: bench
git: networking
shm: 3.0G
### END ENV

### RING-SCALING
rows: 100000
reads: 3 per process
reps: 3 per run, 6 runs interleaved (median reported)
batch: 25000
shape: fp_ring_scaling_t (the benchmark's six-column row)
transport: eventfd
rings_per_worker: <default> (each ring gets its own worker until the server is full; then one worker grows to at most 8 rings)
shm_pool: <default>
git: networking
### END RING-SCALING

 n served ring workers   aggregate rows/s   per reader
 1      1            1          6,325,913    6,325,913
 2      2            2         11,707,960    5,853,980
 4      4            4         21,930,392    5,482,598
 8      8            7         22,640,681    2,830,085
# Eight rings on seven workers: the pool's eighth ring shares a worker instead
# of failing. Raw n=8 values: 22.64M, 22.64M, 22.06M, 22.91M, 22.66M, 22.25M.

### AB
n=1 -3.3% | n=2 -8.2% | n=4 -6.4% | n=8 -27.2%. The change serves 8/8 rings where the baseline serves 7/8; past the budget one worker carries two rings and that straggler sets the wall time. n<=4 is inside this host's +/-5-10% noise (see the same-image control below); n=8 is outside it (six runs each, no overlap).

## Controls

### 1. same image, both engines (cancels build/clock drift)
Six alternations inside one container, n=2, medians:
  shm     12.51M rows/s   (12.57, 11.97, 12.60, 12.43, 12.86, 12.46)
  eventfd 12.58M rows/s   (12.50, 12.63, 11.67, 12.48, 12.85, 12.69)
At n=2 the per-ring path is either the semaphore doorbell or the eventfd pair;
they are the same within noise. The pool does not add a per-read cost when a
ring has its own worker.

### 2. both engines with the budget out of the way (`PG_OPTS=-c max_worker_processes=16`)
Every ring gets a worker of its own, so no multiplexing:
  baseline:  6.36M / 12.90M / 23.98M / 32.93M   (8/8 at n=8)
  change:    6.25M / 11.62M / 23.18M / 32.58M   (8/8 at n=8)
n=8: 32.58M against 32.93M (-1.1%, one pair; a second pair gave 32.58 vs
35.92 with the baseline cell inflated). The eventfd engine is at parity with
the semaphore engine; the n=8 loss in the main table is the shared worker.

### 3. change with overflow disabled (`FASTPOSTGRES_RINGS_PER_WORKER=1`)
The pool behaves as the 1:1 model again:
  1: 6.22M | 2: 11.59M | 4: 22.82M | 8: 31.13M, **7/8 served** (1 had no ring)
That reproduces the baseline's 31.11M and 7/8 exactly, on the eventfd engine;
the entire -27.2% is the one extra ring multiplexed onto an existing worker.

## Reproduce

    docker build -q --target dev -t fpq-networking -f docker/Dockerfile .
    docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e FASTPOSTGRES_TRANSPORT=eventfd \
      -v "$PWD/bench/internals/threads/ring_scaling.py:/tmp/ring_scaling.py:ro" \
      fpq-networking python3 /tmp/ring_scaling.py --rows 100000 --reads 3 --reps 5

`tests.sh` is green on both settings:
  docker run --rm --shm-size=3g fpq-networking tests.sh                        # 55 tests, 1 skipped
  docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e FASTPOSTGRES_TRANSPORT=eventfd fpq-networking tests.sh  # 55 tests, OK

A sanity run of the final committed image (2 reps) reproduced the shape:
6.48M / 11.07M / 22.99M / 22.93M at n=1/2/4/8, 8/8 served, 7 workers at n=8.
